@.str = private unnamed_addr constant [13 x i8] c"RobustKernel\00", align 1
@.str.3 = private unnamed_addr constant [2770 x i8] c"\0ABase class that models a robust kernel for outlier rejection. The virtual\0Afunction ``weight()`` must be implemented in derived classes.\0A\0AThe main idea of a robust loss is to downweight large residuals that are\0Aassumed to be caused from outliers such that their influence on the solution\0Ais reduced. This is achieved by optimizing:\0A\0A.. math::\0A  \\def\\argmin{\\mathop{\\rm argmin}}\0A  \\begin{equation}\0A    x^{*} = \\argmin_{x} \\sum_{i=1}^{N} \\rho({r_i(x)})\0A  \\end{equation}\0A  :label: robust_loss\0A\0Awhere :math:`\\rho(r)` is also called the robust loss or kernel and\0A:math:`r_i(x)` is the residual.\0A\0ASeveral robust kernels have been proposed to deal with different kinds of\0Aoutliers such as Huber, Cauchy, and others.\0A\0AThe optimization problem in :eq:`robust_loss` can be solved using the\0Aiteratively reweighted least squares (IRLS) approach, which solves a sequence\0Aof weighted least squares problems. We can see the relation between the least\0Asquares optimization in stanad non-linear least squares and robust loss\0Aoptimization by comparing the respective gradients which go to zero at the\0Aoptimum (illustrated only for the :math:`i^\\mathrm{th}` residual):\0A\0A.. math::\0A  \\begin{eqnarray}\0A    \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\partial (w_i r^2_i(x))}{\\partial{x}}\0A    &=&\0A    w_i r_i(x) \\frac{\\partial r_i(x)}{\\partial{x}} \\\\\0A    \\label{eq:gradient_ls}\0A    \\frac{\\partial(\\rho(r_i(x)))}{\\partial{x}}\0A    &=&\0A    \\rho'(r_i(x)) \\frac{\\partial r_i(x)}{\\partial{x}}.\0A  \\end{eqnarray}\0A\0ABy setting the weight :math:`w_i= \\frac{1}{r_i(x)}\\rho'(r_i(x))`, we\0Acan solve the robust loss optimization problem by using the existing techniques\0Afor weighted least-squares. This scheme allows standard solvers using\0AGauss-Newton and Levenberg-Marquardt algorithms to optimize for robust losses\0Aand is the one implemented in Open3D.\0A\0AThen we minimize the objective function using Gauss-Newton and determine\0Aincrements by iteratively solving:\0A\0A.. math::\0A  \\newcommand{\\mat}[1]{\\mathbf{#1}}\0A  \\newcommand{\\veca}[1]{\\vec{#1}}\0A  \\renewcommand{\\vec}[1]{\\mathbf{#1}}\0A  \\begin{align}\0A   \\left(\\mat{J}^\\top \\mat{W} \\mat{J}\\right)^{-1}\\mat{J}^\\top\\mat{W}\\vec{r},\0A  \\end{align}\0A\0Awhere :math:`\\mat{W} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n\\times n}` is a diagonal matrix containing\0Aweights :math:`w_i` for each residual :math:`r_i`\0A\0AThe different loss functions will only impact in the weight for each residual\0Aduring the optimization step.\0ATherefore, the only impact of the choice on the kernel is through its first\0Aorder derivate.\0A\0AThe kernels implemented so far, and the notation has been inspired by the\0Apublication: **\22Analysis of Robust Functions for Registration Algorithms\22**, by\0APhilippe Babin et al.\0A\0AFor more information please also see: **\22Adaptive Robust Kernels for\0ANon-Linear Least Squares Problems\22**, by Nived Chebrolu et al.\0A\00", align 1
@.str.4 = private unnamed_addr constant [7 x i8] c"L2Loss\00", align 1
@.str.5 = private unnamed_addr constant [187 x i8] c"\0AThe loss :math:`\\rho(r)` for a given residual ``r`` is given by:\0A\0A.. math:: \\rho(r) = \\frac{r^2}{2}\0A\0AThe weight :math:`w(r)` for a given residual ``r`` is given by:\0A\0A.. math:: w(r) = 1\0A\00", align 1
@.str.6 = private unnamed_addr constant [7 x i8] c"L1Loss\00", align 1
@.str.7 = private unnamed_addr constant [189 x i8] c"\0AThe loss :math:`\\rho(r)` for a given residual ``r`` is given by:\0A\0A.. math:: \\rho(r) = |r|\0A\0AThe weight :math:`w(r)` for a given residual ``r`` is given by:\0A\0A.. math:: w(r) = \\frac{1}{|r|}\0A\00", align 1
@.str.8 = private unnamed_addr constant [10 x i8] c"HuberLoss\00", align 1
@.str.9 = private unnamed_addr constant [474 x i8] c"\0AThe loss :math:`\\rho(r)` for a given residual ``r`` is:\0A\0A.. math::\0A  \\begin{equation}\0A    \\rho(r)=\0A    \\begin{cases}\0A      \\frac{r^{2}}{2}, & |r| \\leq k.\\\\\0A      k(|r|-k / 2), & \\text{otherwise}.\0A    \\end{cases}\0A  \\end{equation}\0A\0AThe weight :math:`w(r)` for a given residual ``r`` is given by:\0A\0A.. math::\0A  \\begin{equation}\0A    w(r)=\0A    \\begin{cases}\0A      1,              & |r| \\leq k.       \\\\\0A      \\frac{k}{|r|} , & \\text{otherwise}.\0A    \\end{cases}\0A  \\end{equation}\0A\00", align 1
@.str.10 = private unnamed_addr constant [11 x i8] c"CauchyLoss\00", align 1
@.str.11 = private unnamed_addr constant [351 x i8] c"\0AThe loss :math:`\\rho(r)` for a given residual ``r`` is:\0A\0A.. math::\0A  \\begin{equation}\0A    \\rho(r)=\0A    \\frac{k^2}{2} \\log\\left(1 + \\left(\\frac{r}{k}\\right)^2\\right)\0A  \\end{equation}\0A\0AThe weight :math:`w(r)` for a given residual ``r`` is given by:\0A\0A.. math::\0A  \\begin{equation}\0A    w(r)=\0A    \\frac{1}{1 + \\left(\\frac{r}{k}\\right)^2}\0A  \\end{equation}\0A\00", align 1
@.str.12 = private unnamed_addr constant [7 x i8] c"GMLoss\00", align 1
@.str.13 = private unnamed_addr constant [304 x i8] c"\0AThe loss :math:`\\rho(r)` for a given residual ``r`` is:\0A\0A.. math::\0A  \\begin{equation}\0A    \\rho(r)=\0A    \\frac{r^2/ 2}{k + r^2}\0A  \\end{equation}\0A\0AThe weight :math:`w(r)` for a given residual ``r`` is given by:\0A\0A.. math::\0A  \\begin{equation}\0A    w(r)=\0A    \\frac{k}{\\left(k + r^2\\right)^2}\0A  \\end{equation}\0A\00", align 1
@.str.14 = private unnamed_addr constant [10 x i8] c"TukeyLoss\00", align 1
@.str.15 = private unnamed_addr constant [658 x i8] c"\0AThe loss :math:`\\rho(r)` for a given residual ``r`` is:\0A\0A.. math::\0A  \\begin{equation}\0A    \\rho(r)=\0A    \\begin{cases}\0A      \\frac{k^2\\left[1-\\left(1-\\left(\\frac{e}{k}\\right)^2\\right)^3\\right]}{2}, & |r| \\leq k.       \\\\\0A      \\frac{k^2}{2},                                                           & \\text{otherwise}.\0A    \\end{cases}\0A  \\end{equation}\0A\0AThe weight :math:`w(r)` for a given residual ``r`` is given by:\0A\0A.. math::\0A  \\begin{equation}\0A    w(r)=\0A    \\begin{cases}\0A      \\left(1 - \\left(\\frac{r}{k}\\right)^2\\right)^2, & |r| \\leq k.       \\\\\0A      0 ,                                            & \\text{otherwise}.\0A    \\end{cases}\0A  \\end{equation}\0A\00", align 1
@.str.16 = private unnamed_addr constant [7 x i8] c"weight\00", align 1
@.str.17 = private unnamed_addr constant [9 x i8] c"residual\00", align 1
